WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.567
Former President

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:01.835
Donald Trump is now facing

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:03.770
mounting threats on multiple fronts.

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:04.604
One week after

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.205
he was arrested

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:05.939
here in New York,

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.107
Trump was back in Manhattan

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:08.441
for a deposition today

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:09.376
with the New York attorney

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:10.443
general Letitia James

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:11.878
as part of her investigation

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.246
into his business.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:14.581
He is also trying to get his trial

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:15.382
for a sexual assault

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:17.117
and defamation postponed.

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:18.385
Meanwhile, in Georgia, the Fulton

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:19.452
County district attorney

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:20.620
is currently weighing

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:21.421
charges over

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:22.589
Trump's push to throw out

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:24.691
the 2020 election results there.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.625
And in Washington,

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.559
there are new signs

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:27.627
that the special counsel's

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.429
probe into January six

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:29.996
and Trump's

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.898
handling of classified documents

00:00:31.898 --> 00:00:32.966
is ramping up.

00:00:32.966 --> 00:00:34.134
Back with me now to discuss

00:00:34.134 --> 00:00:35.001
all of this is Trump's

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:36.603
former attorney general, Bill Barr.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:37.170
Welcome back.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:37.971
I do want to ask you, though,

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:39.239
about the latest legal move

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:40.740
we've seen from your former boss,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.442
which is filing a lawsuit

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:44.244
against his former attorney,

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:45.011
Michael Cohen,

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:47.614
to the tune of $500 million in damages,

00:00:47.847 --> 00:00:48.248
saying that

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.283
he allegedly breached his contract.

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.518
Do you think there's any merit

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:53.086
to that lawsuit?

00:00:53.186 --> 00:00:54.854
I mean, all I've seen is newspaper

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.157
reports on it.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:58.825
I didn't see what the substance

00:00:58.825 --> 00:00:59.859
I didn't detect

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:02.862
any real substance to the lawsuit.

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:05.298
So when I was thinking about this today,

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.033
I was reminded of what the former U.S.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.168
attorney general or U.S.

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:08.501
attorney

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:09.636
in the southern district of New York,

00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:11.771
Jeff Berman, wrote about you in his book.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.473
He said that you tried to kill

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.241
investigations into this

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.310
when it came to true Michael Cohen.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:17.610
He said

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.412
when Barr took over in February, 2019,

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.380
he not only tried to kill

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.015
the ongoing investigations,

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.383
but incredibly suggested

00:01:23.383 --> 00:01:25.051
that Cohen's conviction on campaign

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:27.087
finance charges be reversed.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.288
Is that true?

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:29.389
Well, first,

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:30.156
he was he

00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:31.791
he pled guilty before I arrived

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.593
at the Department of Justice.

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.762
And I didn't kill any investigation.

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:37.797
But

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:38.798
you know,

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:40.800
I think people did come to the conclusion

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:44.604
that even assuming the facts about

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:46.573
Trump were were correct,

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:48.041
that it would not have constitute an

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.212
illegal political campaign contribution.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:53.646
But I didn't try to

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.081
kill any investigation.

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.015
And in fact,

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:56.483
you know,

00:01:56.483 --> 00:01:58.952
I was gone from the Department of Justice

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:01.121
now for two years.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:03.123
It's under the Biden administration.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.190
If they thought that there was

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.158
something there,

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.326
they were free to bring it.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:07.560
They were also free to

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.728
you know, to raise it with me.

00:02:08.728 --> 00:02:09.963
And no one ever did.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.331
Did you pursuing a case,

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:12.298
did you suggest that

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.800
Cohen's conviction on campaign

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.035
finance charges be reversed?

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:18.037
No. He pled guilty.

00:02:18.037 --> 00:02:18.638
I didn't.

00:02:18.705 --> 00:02:21.474
I felt the plea should stand.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:21.941
I want to move on

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:22.976
to the other investigations,

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:23.576
because today

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:24.210
we also saw

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:25.278
one of your former colleagues,

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:25.612
the former

00:02:25.612 --> 00:02:27.180
director of National Intelligence,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.015
John Ratcliffe, go and testify

00:02:29.015 --> 00:02:30.383
before the grand jury in D.C.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.918
That's investigating,

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:32.519
of course, Trump's

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.120
actions on January six.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.421
You've said that you believe

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:37.857
your former boss has dug himself a hole

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:40.193
when it comes to this investigation

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.628
and the documents investigation.

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:42.529
What do you mean by that when it

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:43.863
comes to the January 61?

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:45.632
No.

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:47.433
What I was saying was, you know,

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:48.768
I try to call him as I see him.

00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:49.936
And there are some cases

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:51.671
or some legal proceedings

00:02:51.671 --> 00:02:53.439
that I think have been unjust

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:54.541
and unfair to Trump,

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:56.943
including the Russiagate episode.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.478
And the New York case

00:02:58.478 --> 00:02:59.913
is both the civil case

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:02.182
and the latest criminal case.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:03.583
I don't think they have any substance,

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:04.417
and I think they're broad

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:07.020
for political reasons.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.521
But that doesn't mean everything

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.823
that Trump does. He's a victim on.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:12.425
And I said he's frequently

00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:13.426
his own worst enemy

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:14.627
and he digs himself holes

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:15.028
and he does

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.563
some things that are reckless,

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:18.932
that are clearly going to

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.466
give rise to investigations

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:21.301
and look into them.

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:22.969
And that included

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:25.738
both the documents in Mar a Lago and

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:29.209
the January 6th episode

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.478
and the events after December 14th

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.813
and trying to

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:34.981
reverse the election

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:35.882
when all the states

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.017
had certified their votes.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:39.652
Do you think Pence's testimony

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.788
could potentially be damaging

00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:43.022
to Trump or game changing

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.190
for that investigation?

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:46.659
I mean,

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:47.994
obviously, they

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:48.728
have fought hard

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.697
to get the testimony of people

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:51.364
who were in direct

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:55.168
contact with Trump and potentially

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:56.836
be their information.

00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.239
Their evidence can be

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:01.507
can be crucial to making a case

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:02.742
if there is one to be made.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.844
I I've been a little skeptical

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:06.112
about them able

00:04:06.112 --> 00:04:08.548
to establish a case on January six,

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:09.983
but I don't know all the facts.

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:11.217
I've said all along,

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.620
I think the more significant case

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.788
or the one that appears to me to be

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:17.290
most threatening

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.292
are the moral of the document case.

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:20.193
Why do you think that one's

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.762
more more threatening to him?

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.064
Because, you know, when it first came out

00:04:25.064 --> 00:04:26.065
that he had the documents,

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:27.667
a lot of people sort of immediately

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:28.701
ran and saying, you know,

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:30.703
why didn't the government seek them,

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:31.638
talk to him about it?

00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:33.206
Why didn't the government subpoena it?

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:33.740
Why did they

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:34.774
why they had the raid

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.010
and so forth or the search?

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:37.744
And it turns out,

00:04:37.744 --> 00:04:38.878
as I suggested,

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.813
that they jawbone them for a year

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:41.748
and a half.

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:44.050
They did subpoena him

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.586
And I think the real question

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:47.053
there is not

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.554
whether he kept the documents

00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:50.323
and had them in Mar a Lago

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:53.059
so much as once this was raised with him.

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:54.193
And it was clear

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:55.194
that he was being asked

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:56.462
to return the documents

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.331
as the government's property.

00:04:58.331 --> 00:04:59.799
The games were played

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:01.100
for quite a long time,

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.303
and I think that that exposes him.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:04.804
Do you think?

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:05.538
Yeah.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:06.873
Do you think he can be charged

00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:08.141
on obstruction

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:10.009
only here, or does there need to be

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:11.744
an underlying crime, in your view?

00:05:14.847 --> 00:05:16.082
I think in this

00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:18.685
case, the the underlying offense

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:22.322
was his having these documents,

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:23.656
which he shouldn't have had,

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.925
and he was subpoenaed.

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:27.660
And if he doesn't provide them

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.929
and hides them from the government,

00:05:29.929 --> 00:05:32.432
there's both an underlying

00:05:32.965 --> 00:05:33.633
offense

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:35.702
and there's the offense of obstruction.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:38.271
But the thing that I think actually

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:39.806
brings this,

00:05:39.806 --> 00:05:41.040
you know, that raises this

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.575
and makes it a more significant

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:45.478
threat is the obstruction aspect of it.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.681
And I you know, I'm not saying, you know,

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:51.050
I just think there's a high risk here

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.352
and just based

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.954
on the government's conduct here,

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:58.558
I suspect that they have some evidence of

00:05:58.791 --> 00:05:58.958
that.

00:05:58.958 --> 00:05:59.792
They would consider it

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:01.861
to be strong evidence of obstruction

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:02.929
and that's why

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:03.363
I feel

00:06:03.363 --> 00:06:03.930
that this is

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.265
probably the most threatening case.

00:06:06.399 --> 00:06:07.333
What do you mean by that

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:07.967
based on what

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:08.868
the government's doing here?

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:09.202
Do you mean

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:11.304
because of how aggressively Jackson

00:06:11.604 --> 00:06:13.506
investigation is progressing?

00:06:13.506 --> 00:06:15.441
The fact that he was able

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:16.309
to talk to Evan

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:17.343
Corcoran, one of Trump's

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:18.211
defense attorneys,

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:19.779
without the shield of attorney

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:20.413
client privilege,

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:20.880
what is it

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.715
that that makes you realize

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:24.183
that it might be a serious case

00:06:24.183 --> 00:06:25.351
for your former boss?

00:06:25.351 --> 00:06:26.552
Well, they did

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:27.720
you know, they did puncture

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.187
the attorney

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.756
client privilege, as you said,

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:31.958
through the so so-called crime fraud

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:33.960
establishing before the judge

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:35.428
the crime fraud exception,

00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:36.129
which was to say

00:06:36.129 --> 00:06:37.530
there was probable cause that

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:41.701
there was evidence of a crime here.

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:42.635
So

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.539
that and, you know, just

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:48.241
the things I read in the newspaper

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:49.075
make me feel that

00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:50.910
there's probably a likelihood

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.245
that they have people,

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:54.247
people who have cooperated

00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:55.214
with the government

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.750
and may be able to establish that.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:58.951
He well knew

00:06:58.951 --> 00:06:59.886
he had not delivered

00:06:59.886 --> 00:07:03.022
all the documents back to the government.

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:04.857
It's a pretty extraordinary case

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.092
if they do move forward with it.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:07.527
If you were the attorney general,

00:07:07.527 --> 00:07:08.594
do you think

00:07:08.995 --> 00:07:09.929
you could feel comfortable

00:07:09.929 --> 00:07:10.830
moving forward

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.032
with indicting a former president?

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:14.734
Well, you know,

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:16.636
I think that this is the kind of case

00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:19.305
that requires a lot of discretion,

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:21.274
a prosecutorial discretion.

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:22.308
And one of the things that I think

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:24.410
is appropriate to consider is, you know,

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:26.245
what would this do to the body politic?

00:07:27.513 --> 00:07:29.682
You know, the precedent it sets and

00:07:29.782 --> 00:07:30.683
and having to deal

00:07:30.683 --> 00:07:32.452
with the question of uneven justice,

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.320
people will point to Hillary Clinton

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:34.854
and so forth.

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:36.489
And those are legitimate points

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:37.290
to consider.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:38.558
And I'm you know, I'm not paid

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.026
the big bucks to make that call.

00:07:40.026 --> 00:07:40.526
Right now,

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:43.329
so I'm not going to offer my views on it.

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:44.297
But

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:46.399
one of the things I've said

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:48.401
is that the appointment of Jack Smith

00:07:49.202 --> 00:07:51.737
makes me feel that they have decided

00:07:52.038 --> 00:07:54.373
not to

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:56.642
That if the facts are there

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.044
to sustain a case,

00:07:58.044 --> 00:07:59.612
that it's a supportable case

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:01.547
that would ordinarily be indicted,

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:02.715
that they've made the decision

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:04.417
to indict it

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:06.686
and that they're not going to tank

00:08:06.686 --> 00:08:07.220
the case

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:08.621
because of

00:08:08.621 --> 00:08:10.823
these discretionary considerations.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.391
That's now this has

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:13.893
you know, that was my view before

00:08:15.328 --> 00:08:16.362
they found

00:08:16.362 --> 00:08:16.929
the president

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:18.664
Biden had taken some documents

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:20.800
and Vice President Pence.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.435
And that complicates things.

00:08:22.435 --> 00:08:23.836
So, you know,

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:25.171
we'll see where it falls out.

00:08:25.171 --> 00:08:26.038
But again,

00:08:26.038 --> 00:08:27.273
I do think that

00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:29.308
that's the most serious thing facing the

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:30.810
the former president.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:31.978
And you see the distinction

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:32.845
because obviously

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.213
the obstruction

00:08:34.213 --> 00:08:34.881
factor is

00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:37.049
what's at play with Trump's situation.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:37.683
That's not something

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:38.451
that's been brought up

00:08:38.451 --> 00:08:39.585
when it comes to Biden

00:08:39.585 --> 00:08:40.653
or when it comes to Pence.

00:08:40.653 --> 00:08:41.354
I do want to ask you,

00:08:41.354 --> 00:08:41.888
because Trump's

00:08:41.888 --> 00:08:43.990
not just facing these investigations

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:44.624
obviously he's facing

00:08:44.624 --> 00:08:45.491
the one here in New York,

00:08:45.491 --> 00:08:48.394
maybe one an indictment and in Georgia.

00:08:48.427 --> 00:08:49.462
Do you believe that

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:50.930
presidents and former presidents

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:53.099
should be categorically immune

00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:54.333
from being prosecuted

00:08:54.333 --> 00:08:56.102
by these elected state

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:58.471
and local prosecutors?

00:08:58.471 --> 00:08:59.906
What's your what's your sense of that?

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:02.041
Well, obviously, they're not

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:04.277
categorically immune from it.

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:04.610
I mean,

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.112
if the former president goes out

00:09:06.112 --> 00:09:06.579
and commits

00:09:06.579 --> 00:09:08.614
a crime in a particular state, a clear

00:09:09.382 --> 00:09:10.349
crime, then

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:10.816
then they're not

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:12.485
immune from prosecution simply

00:09:12.485 --> 00:09:14.687
because they're the former president.

00:09:14.687 --> 00:09:17.189
But this is something that really

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:20.526
is completely hanging on a federal law,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.728
a federal offense.

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:24.530
And

00:09:25.131 --> 00:09:26.699
this is a local prosecutor

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:27.867
doesn't have jurisdiction

00:09:27.867 --> 00:09:28.901
over the federal offense.

00:09:28.901 --> 00:09:29.702
And he's basically

00:09:29.702 --> 00:09:30.403
premised on

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:32.071
this is that the president

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:34.507
violated a federal law but

00:09:34.507 --> 00:09:36.108
the president would be entitled

00:09:36.108 --> 00:09:37.043
to have that determined

00:09:37.043 --> 00:09:39.512
by a federal court and a federal jury.

00:09:39.979 --> 00:09:42.915
And and so that's the problem here,

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:44.350
is that he's

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:45.284
for clearly,

00:09:45.284 --> 00:09:47.219
I think clearly partizan reasons.

00:09:47.219 --> 00:09:48.087
He's dredged up

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:49.989
this offense

00:09:49.989 --> 00:09:52.091
and is trying to bootstrap it

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:53.559
into a felony

00:09:53.559 --> 00:09:55.494
and go after the president with it.

00:09:55.494 --> 00:09:57.096
And the federal government

00:09:57.096 --> 00:09:58.364
has never pursued this

00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:00.333
either.

00:10:00.333 --> 00:10:01.834
When I was in office, as far,

00:10:01.834 --> 00:10:02.068
you know,

00:10:02.068 --> 00:10:03.069
they never brought it to me

00:10:03.069 --> 00:10:04.604
as a potential case

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:05.605
against the president.

00:10:05.605 --> 00:10:06.439
And after I left,

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:06.839
it hasn't

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:08.908
been raised as a potential offense.

00:10:08.908 --> 00:10:10.376
So if the federal government isn't,

00:10:10.376 --> 00:10:10.910
what would that be?

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:12.912
Because if he's a sitting president,

00:10:12.912 --> 00:10:16.215
the idea that he couldn't be indicted.

00:10:16.215 --> 00:10:17.984
No, I said after I left.

00:10:17.984 --> 00:10:20.987
So after the president left office,

00:10:20.987 --> 00:10:22.955
no one has as far as I'm aware,

00:10:22.955 --> 00:10:24.056
the Department of Justice

00:10:24.056 --> 00:10:26.192
is not pursuing this case,

00:10:26.192 --> 00:10:28.794
because the problem with the case

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:31.664
technically is that

00:10:31.664 --> 00:10:32.732
the payment of hush

00:10:32.732 --> 00:10:35.368
money is not a campaign contribution.

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:38.738
It's a payment of a personal expense.

00:10:39.405 --> 00:10:40.740
And the issue then becomes

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:41.307
whether it would have

00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:43.809
been made irrespective of the campaign.

00:10:44.377 --> 00:10:44.810
And that would

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.345
be very tough to show here.

00:10:46.345 --> 00:10:47.880
So I don't think I don't think it is

00:10:49.181 --> 00:10:50.249
a federal offense.

